1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,199
Derived repository method names run out of
breath. "Find manifests for vessel
IMO-9312345 with arrival date on
2026-09-18" is still readable as a method.
Add three more predicates and the name
becomes a novel.

2
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:32,615
JPQL lets you query the entity model —
property names, associations — without
writing table-column SQL by hand. JPQL
looks like SQL but speaks entities. You
write select m from Manifest m, not select
* from manifests.

3
00:00:32,736 --> 00:00:50,136
Fields are Java properties. Associations
are navigated with dots or joins.
Hibernate translates to SQL for your
dialect. Look at this java for a moment.
Parameter binding uses :name with @Param,
or ordinal ?1 style.

4
00:00:50,256 --> 00:01:02,472
Prefer named parameters for harbor queries
you will revisit at 3am. join m.lines
navigates the association you mapped — you
did not type cargo_lines.manifest_id.

5
00:01:02,591 --> 00:01:14,280
Projection queries return what a screen
needs without hydrating full graphs: Look
at this java for a moment. @Modifying on
an update/delete JPQL query marks it as a
write.

6
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:22,896
Bulk JPQL updates bypass the persistence
context’s dirty checking for already-
managed instances — clear the context if
you mixed styles.

7
00:01:23,016 --> 00:01:35,736
Native SQL with nativeQuery = true is an
escape hatch when you need a database-
specific hint. Prefer JPQL while the query
stays in entity terms; drop to native when
the dialect feature is the point.

8
00:01:35,856 --> 00:01:48,480
Read the vessel-and-day query against a
real ops question: "show me everything for
IMO-9312345 arriving today, ordered by
manifest number." The JPQL states that in
entity language.

9
00:01:48,600 --> 00:02:01,104
Hibernate emits the SQL with the real
column names for your dialect. When you
rename arrivalDate in Java, the query
updates with the model — not with a buried
arrival_date string in twelve native
queries.

10
00:02:01,224 --> 00:02:15,120
Writing table columns inside JPQL
(manifest_number) is a common failure —
JPQL wants manifestNumber. Selecting
entities and then filtering in Java loops
is how N+1 and memory problems start.

11
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:25,296
And treating JPQL as "string SQL with
different keywords" misses the point:
refactor a property name and your JPQL
should follow the model, not the physical
schema.

12
00:02:25,415 --> 00:02:29,807
JPQL is great when the query shape is
known at compile time.

13
00:02:29,927 --> 00:02:40,175
When port-search filters appear and
disappear at runtime — optional flag
state, optional tonnage range, optional
name fragment — string-concatenated JPQL
gets unsafe and ugly.

14
00:02:40,295 --> 00:02:44,783
The Criteria API builds those predicates
programmatically.
